#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/4e47cfcf3da66707e6bc898d099868bfbd6d5163dcd38d47e4c79b2180e89ec6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f57136-53c9-5d5b-81a3-d361f456559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333967} {:answer/id #uuid "3622aad5-b996-5d34-9f22-6444517decc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e824e3f-6818-52e6-8e93-cf30bbce3ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333969} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcdb2b0-87a5-52ad-8fdc-940d513c7609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333970} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1794e4-5a4f-5d99-a469-93b0685a8f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e824e3f-6818-52e6-8e93-cf30bbce3ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333969} :answer-field/id #uuid "982f2593-50a7-5034-a02e-038a5a3354b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "108e2d2a-b886-54f8-90a0-21329e09bdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161079" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5c19ff-cf95-5829-9048-fc6f350d87ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333974} {:answer/id #uuid "a8609207-33a8-5098-87fb-0c378d57ea16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333975} {:answer/id #uuid "b2418142-2ffc-5ddf-ad74-97241b6d50ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333976} {:answer/id #uuid "29f6505d-ab92-514b-91a5-2e557547eaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333977} {:answer/id #uuid "04f650e9-ac51-5c7a-bf2d-7d393d70b95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f6505d-ab92-514b-91a5-2e557547eaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333977} :answer-field/id #uuid "5764dc4b-51b4-53ba-879b-0fbb9f545772" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d08ae8b-d701-5c32-a8bc-6cc3936a7567" :question/math-academy-id "q-161101" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=x+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/b9c2cd48302ef8d4e2d5e65a56d19506600c5e6552a4d9a1ef7d578a09a21557.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1af7024-2d9e-5ffe-819c-acdf20bc6222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153858} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e3ea3c-1449-5822-91d5-ebfaa712eb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153859} {:answer/id #uuid "26676f10-9bd7-509f-8ee0-3099f3bd2978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153860} {:answer/id #uuid "33044875-6540-506f-8bf7-2e868a877e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153861} {:answer/id #uuid "5061f29d-83ee-5a8f-acfd-e15140b98ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33044875-6540-506f-8bf7-2e868a877e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153861} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f17e940-a965-5325-b128-00fa2d86e1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2616ffa6-4ad5-55b3-bbe1-ee00aff863bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161102" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/b0e8a196dc27f333db4179b5c318ccd3aa4b385c0752c570fbfedc0226b3b15b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbcd4135-5629-597f-b35b-f780cb5be53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333981} {:answer/id #uuid "a614c07f-aaa8-5fef-acd1-5a4453286ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333982} {:answer/id #uuid "4e053cd7-87c9-52d3-8367-295e2dcf20e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{272}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333983} {:answer/id #uuid "03800d38-5328-501e-aec6-b4c8be727556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333984} {:answer/id #uuid "88a6da60-d7c1-5cf3-b0ae-36e8364d3db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{136}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e053cd7-87c9-52d3-8367-295e2dcf20e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{272}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333983} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6cc85e6-2d6d-5944-9612-8d5244a6aa5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b6cfed2-365c-5005-ae47-027cb53be6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161121" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the half-annulus between the disks of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ we obtain $r=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/da18aafec278b5d44b0530097729e4cb4b319e219c005fc155d78fab8aa90937.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta <\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{5}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{81}{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =136{[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =136\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{272}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bba07f2-4794-55af-a772-8be2f0978451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155921} {:answer/id #uuid "919e14e2-8417-5f72-a729-cd2d37a0937f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{0}^{4}r^{2}drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155922} {:answer/id #uuid "2150c20a-648e-53fc-8cad-192160a17305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155923} {:answer/id #uuid "282f2098-8f08-5d6d-8e4a-68be5e9aa942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155924} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2cc414-abcd-507f-8635-ea1f78b3723a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sec \\theta }^{4\\cos \\theta }rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2150c20a-648e-53fc-8cad-192160a17305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155923} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e06f359-1f76-59b3-9610-e06ae6076faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a9bf64-a4cc-56e6-b47b-b81ac55fade6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333988} {:answer/id #uuid "789dd74b-cdfa-5805-98a5-1f4d3f8d2eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333989} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e5ba0e-b9ef-59e3-80e1-1f6f01400644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333990} {:answer/id #uuid "ad893a91-f9e0-5ce7-883c-67be6b98622b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333991} {:answer/id #uuid "39cbb040-bc78-5875-b3b7-90433fc3dd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789dd74b-cdfa-5805-98a5-1f4d3f8d2eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333989} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d1359b5-6ade-5f8d-890b-f8b13f918ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "939fc921-a379-5688-8de2-0d74998d81dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83999" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/d0d72be437a462f5eea781e83cc2a95e956e11ddc3295ad3b02a4a57867d9271.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(\\sqrt{3},-1).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/2386c1dcb1cf71231f2bb508dc1dd85dadef15b806eaf7f9a9ad0d3e4f3da1ca.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{6}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /6} \\\\  & =[(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(0-\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcb979f-3921-508f-9b2b-ae8e7145b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155975} {:answer/id #uuid "0eef47cb-4788-507e-b1f4-c3795d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r^{2}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155976} {:answer/id #uuid "2033d184-d234-5f04-a79e-49eafcb830da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{2}(\\sin \\theta  - \\cos \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155977} {:answer/id #uuid "2274961c-375d-58ac-94ad-7773f6f452f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}r^{3}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155978} {:answer/id #uuid "751a9151-32fb-5553-b22a-f8cb9eecd5c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{4}r(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eef47cb-4788-507e-b1f4-c3795d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r^{2}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155976} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ce5ea4-7b87-505a-8ba6-b0fd5e7fa28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97fc9bc2-d10b-5e4f-be32-39965512311e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85059" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/6f7f37b10d483f7ce2bf147f316195b4be578ae62da8538a3aac4a8dbfa2ec1c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8606fca-6c91-5ce3-8b54-4885efa31bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333995} {:answer/id #uuid "d47bf0e0-034a-5c33-a0a4-002005b20e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333996} {:answer/id #uuid "13b21b53-54ae-5196-ac87-89a7aa1ed4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333997} {:answer/id #uuid "945bc7a3-7d7a-59ad-913b-4b718bd7deea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333998} {:answer/id #uuid "bfddaf45-41e4-5b5e-ab06-3379de163327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{9}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8606fca-6c91-5ce3-8b54-4885efa31bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333995} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0e588a-bb87-54c6-9f52-cd84bb489729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b47b34a-1974-5599-b1be-0d4b61ed5472" :question/math-academy-id "q-85060" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/0d91ac9127718b0f2755ec14c268d23af810c2ec6f13b4935930b4b348b76b34.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{ }dA & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb1c558-ce13-5f56-bdc1-35bc2cf7730f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3}^{5}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{-\\pi /2}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334002} {:answer/id #uuid "390208c7-2e35-56bf-87c9-14bbc62811bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334003} {:answer/id #uuid "556f7ea8-650b-5724-b395-545682970260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334004} {:answer/id #uuid "00de5bad-7cdc-5abd-aa19-1d454d2e081a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334005} {:answer/id #uuid "b83119d4-d638-5c55-8d46-eac9f9a8762c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{9}^{25}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00de5bad-7cdc-5abd-aa19-1d454d2e081a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334005} :answer-field/id #uuid "28183cea-c6c0-504f-90f1-d21f31604e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cfa5c22-7854-5e49-ad77-545303651a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/ec2b27e99ad03df281464b1a1fb08b5e8cbf869acf1892a815d9031bf506dafe.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the third quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 5,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{5}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9e3390d-9a72-50e8-bede-151e4947904d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334009} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2f9c47-64f8-5cd4-8291-1a4ca7ec17cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334010} {:answer/id #uuid "b45830b4-0744-5cfd-a282-a0603554de28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /6}{\\int }_{-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334011} {:answer/id #uuid "7806ad45-3da7-5aa5-a036-d036f4e13559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334012} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9ee4eb-4a8f-5e8a-a6c4-c4f83cebad53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9ee4eb-4a8f-5e8a-a6c4-c4f83cebad53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334013} :answer-field/id #uuid "1899ac13-8bd0-57fa-b2c3-c40a582918ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bddde3a-859e-58d0-9562-df6a6a576260" :question/math-academy-id "q-85062" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/acb2f50d01df6ebec6003cc7b9c944b759113b9f364455fb62eede389b335229.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-\\sqrt{3},-3)$ and $(\\sqrt{3},-3).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/5991463a56d8358164d32b15f73e938573463d8d706d2b9c8fc06803326a5f0f.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-2\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2\\pi /3}^{-\\pi /3}{\\int }_{-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e204d77a-255f-5eec-9466-70c3fd2bd48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334016} {:answer/id #uuid "df7150db-979e-5a1d-875b-4b73a9f4806f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334017} {:answer/id #uuid "77ecddd3-28de-5a61-b2d7-b749f8387b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334018} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a0cfd5-1119-5025-bc84-f3c518bbc354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{1}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334019} {:answer/id #uuid "4a042657-2b55-58a3-847a-c5a36437f2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df7150db-979e-5a1d-875b-4b73a9f4806f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334017} :answer-field/id #uuid "3139e0e1-17d2-5c9e-b85c-96c6e707290b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94aee767-7dc5-5c61-be85-11041ceb750d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85067" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/dc35d0362ef95d11402ad44cded01abf46686d3088e9d852bd0b9a7ba2454e16.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi ,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/ca09f228fc218ef1b57196c6b6b1e740c10173b7cd38b1c27407726eccd783f1.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}}\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta /\\sqrt{2}}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec658ba7-e967-53d2-b7a5-46ef3845b9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334023} {:answer/id #uuid "24fecbcd-671e-5be7-8f3d-4d81ab09bddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334024} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b29140-5283-5e62-b4ee-c41f0020750a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334025} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a657e5-a5af-57ad-af07-4c7ea7556e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334026} {:answer/id #uuid "7602d930-bd07-50d5-839b-8dafc8b23da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec658ba7-e967-53d2-b7a5-46ef3845b9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334023} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea87252-16b5-5057-9ca7-d8e94d8d267d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7058f657-11d2-5523-91ce-8187030a00b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-85177" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/97dfdde016e4c3482fe036379e22aa95af27c1bcf93076ea9054e263100ad8e1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09fa82b-f6ce-55f8-8aaa-4ed16725fb62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334030} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ae53ac-ece4-5e32-9274-27693c9942c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334031} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e5c03-69bd-5bcc-98d5-c67fe9f8b68c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334032} {:answer/id #uuid "336d5bf2-8491-50f7-8dd5-b20cd2e60752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334033} {:answer/id #uuid "cbea9882-f2d4-5070-a77b-27824dc1647b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbea9882-f2d4-5070-a77b-27824dc1647b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334034} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9741a1-096b-5dea-8215-2114e0d558e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd504b15-bc52-5a08-812b-fde09422adac" :question/math-academy-id "q-85182" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/51439321ae9d3760eb48d54351dbd519efe290c77d9197ca55aa590ce79e91a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the line $x=3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3 \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/bf2d20acbb93d3c61e9890a49d1c15a08c2b4e18b474da421bded7cf3dafc297.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}{\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{0}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{3\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddacb08e-ad82-53d2-ac7f-c049bc330e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334037} {:answer/id #uuid "c36785ed-045f-51a6-8b92-9a58654176bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334038} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d79de3-7bb2-5eda-9290-1df112a63404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334039} {:answer/id #uuid "8d38fea8-c8fe-5682-b61d-6d4a9ac0ab12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334040} {:answer/id #uuid "422b5cf8-a06b-5621-b3c8-3d63da0bb4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5d79de3-7bb2-5eda-9290-1df112a63404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334039} :answer-field/id #uuid "94034ac5-6db5-5120-a36f-bd11e45979ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d97c4f3-ec20-5090-97d9-495e8f3aa9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85204" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-12\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155180f4-d19a-5aff-9529-b2cde64bd4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334044} {:answer/id #uuid "2f112108-fc29-5944-b533-b4da8475ffb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334045} {:answer/id #uuid "c08bf500-b4aa-54f9-a809-fb58f01d0092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334046} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc8ba26-c416-5083-b24b-b9e2a596de61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334047} {:answer/id #uuid "f02dd304-8613-5e88-a981-a78f6e4032b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f02dd304-8613-5e88-a981-a78f6e4032b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334048} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4b75d4-b828-549b-b05e-2d2e1004592c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3911b601-a237-5fb0-be5d-bd15dffefda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-85206" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/bf7089294bc72330065590a9fe54acebee6d0ab982bffb4a162407c7232898ec.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056707/assets/1b83e2674bba4bd6c326083f8c94496b5288de23575dc63a92b9a660eba69f67.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
